tings and markets near 97 percent confirm the race is not competitive.", "watch": "Only a major Moore scandal or debate collapse paired with a late national shift toward Republicans could narrow the race, and even that would likely change the margin more than the winner.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 63 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Wes Moore (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Wes Moore seeks a second term against Dan Cox, the Republican he defeated in 2022. Maryland's 15-point Democratic lean, Moore's incumbency, and a lopsided money race frame the contest: he has raised about $15.5 million with $6.5 million on hand, while Cox has raised roughly $242,000. An August poll showed Moore ahead 58 to 31, and earlier surveys also found double-digit leads.\n\nEvery major race rater places the contest in the solid or safe Democratic column, and prediction markets put Moore's chances near 97 percent. Cox has campaigned on electricity costs and the economy, and the candidates met in an October debate, but little public evidence suggests a competitive race. The open question is the size of the margin, not the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9967, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9958, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9942, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9961, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9947, "margin": 20.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9966, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9972, "margin": 23.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 20.0}]}